course: dictionary
lesson: 3523
title: Favouritism and fairness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Favouritism means treating one person or group better than others, and it is unfair. Ask yourself: is this fair to everyone? If one pupil is always picked and the others never are, that is favouritism.
explain: «Fair ways to share something scarce include drawing lots, a waiting list and helping those in greatest need; choosing your friends is favouritism.» Scarce means there is not enough to go round. Drawing lots, a waiting list and helping those in greatest need are fair. Choosing your friends first is not.
explain: Stories show how favouritism hurts. In the story of Joseph, his father gave him a special coat, and his brothers became jealous. Words to know: «show favouritism», «accuse someone of favouritism», «no favouritism».
fact: Favouritism means unfairly treating one person or group better than others.
fact: Favouritism is a noun. In American English it is spelt «favoritism».
fact: Favouritism often causes jealousy in the people who are left out.
fact: Fair adults try to show «no favouritism» in school and in sport.
fact: To «accuse someone of favouritism» means to say they are treating one person unfairly.
exercise: What does favouritism mean? | Winning every race you enter | *Unfairly treating one person better than others | Giving everyone exactly the same share of the cake | Being kind to animals
exercise: «Choosing your friends is favouritism.» Why is this? | *It is unfair to the people you do not choose | It means you are a good friend to everyone | It is always the fairest way to share | It is the same as drawing lots
exercise: Which is a fair way to share something scarce? | Choosing your best friends first | *Drawing lots | Keeping it all for yourself | Giving it only to the loudest child
test: Joseph's father gave him a special coat. What did this cause among his brothers? | Sleep | Hunger | *Jealousy | Happiness
test: A teacher always picks the same pupil to answer questions. What is this? | Fair sharing | Good context | *Favouritism | Kindness
exam: Which sentence uses favouritism correctly? | Joseph's father showed favouritism by giving everyone a coat | Showing no favouritism is a cruel thing to do | Favouritism means sharing things equally with everyone | *A parent who gives one child much more than the others is showing favouritism
